## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
<h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # So Many Different Historical Events to Learn! – Lesson # 1</h1>
<p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> So Many Different Historical Events to Learn!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify a simple chronological sequence in a recorded narrative or dialogue. Interactive: Respond to and clarify multipart questions.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify chronological events in a historical narrative or recorded interview about Panama (e.g., "What happened after the Torrijos-Carter Treaties were signed?"). Can answer multipart questions about a podcast on key historical events with clarification as needed (e.g., "Who signed the Torrijos-Carter Treaties, and what were the outcomes?"). Can listen to a historical podcast or documentary clip and list in order key events related to Panama's history (e.g., "First, the treaty was negotiated, then Panama regained control of the canal in 1999.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of iconic historical images related to Panama (e.g., separation from Colombia, canal construction, canal transfer, indigenous groups). Students work in pairs to brainstorm what they know about these 'events.' Teacher asks: "What do you see in these pictures? What 'historical events' do they represent? What does 'history' mean to you?" (3-5 min)</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 core vocabulary words on the board (e.g., <i>history, Panama, independence, canal, construction, economy, events, transformation, legacy, heritage</i>). Teacher models pronunciation, focusing on stress for nouns like '<b>hist</b>ory' and '-ed' endings in past simple verbs such as 'declar<b>ed</b>' /d/, 'accomplish<b>ed</b>' /t/, 'suggest<b>ed</b>' /ɪd/. Teacher also models contrastive sounds, for example: /s/ in "say" and /z/ in "was," /ð/ in "they" and /θ/ in "think." (5-7 min)</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> Teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'independence' when a country is free or controlled by another?" (Expected: free)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'construction' mean building something new or taking it apart?" (Expected: building something new)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something has a 'legacy,' does it mean it will be remembered in the future, or forgotten?" (Expected: remembered)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Research History"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) recorded podcast segment or documentary narration about the construction and early impact of the Panama Canal. The audio uses past simple, passive voice, and several target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>canal, construction, economy, engineering, transformation, began, completed</i>).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify a simple chronological sequence in a recorded narrative. Listening: Interactive: Respond to questions about the content.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen once and identify the main topic. Teacher asks: "What is this podcast segment mainly about?" (Expected: The Panama Canal's construction).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and answer True/False questions related to key facts and chronological events (e.g., "The canal was completed before 1900. (False)", "The construction had a big impact on Panama's economy. (True)").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Sequencing):</b> Students listen for specific chronological markers. Teacher provides a jumbled list of 3-4 key events mentioned in the audio (e.g., Construction began, First ship passed, Disease challenged workers, It transformed global routes). Students listen and put them in the correct order.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, Grammar and Vocabulary</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details, past simple for historical events, passive voice, reported speech, and target vocabulary (e.g., <i>declared, completed, began, transformed, significance, engineering</i>).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Fill-in-the-blanks):</b> Teacher distributes a handout with a partial transcript of the audio, containing gaps for target vocabulary words and key verbs in the past simple or passive voice (e.g., "The Panama Canal ______ (complete) in 1914. Its ______ (construct) was a major ______ (engineer) marvel. Historians ______ (report) it ______ (transform) global trade routes."). Students listen to the audio again (segment by segment) and fill in the blanks. (5 min)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Reported Speech & Pronunciation):</b> Teacher plays specific sentences from the audio. Students, in pairs, rephrase these sentences using reported speech, practicing the shift in tense. For example, Teacher plays: "The narrator said, 'The canal began in 1904.'" Students: "The narrator said the canal *had begun* in 1904." Teacher emphasizes pronunciation of /s/ vs /z/ in words like "was" and "said," and /ð/ vs /θ/ if applicable in the sentences. They also focus on the stress of two-syllable nouns vs. verbs like "research" (noun: <b>re</b>search, verb: re<b>search</b>). (5 min)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Chronological Summary):</b> In pairs, students use their completed handouts to orally summarize the historical events from the audio in chronological order, using their own words and practicing the target vocabulary and past simple verbs. They ask each other clarifying questions if they disagree on the sequence or details (e.g., "When did Panama *declare* its independence? What *happened* after the canal *was completed*?"). (5 min)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Research History"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Historical News Report" (oral presentation).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a *new* short (1-1.5 min) audio clip about a different significant Panamanian historical event (e.g., Panama's separation from Colombia or the Torrijos-Carter Treaties).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups listen attentively, taking notes on key events, dates, and people.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In their groups, students collaboratively discuss and summarize the historical event, focusing on establishing a clear chronological sequence. They must use at least 5 of the new vocabulary words and practice past simple and passive voice. They also practice asking each other for clarification on facts or interpretations (e.g., "When did Panama separate from Colombia?", "What impact did the construction of the Panama Canal have on the country's economy?").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then presents a brief "news report" (2-3 minutes) of the event to another group, detailing the sequence of events. They act as "historical reporters" who have "learned" to "research history" by listening.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative Assessment and Reflection</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group news reports.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group's oral "Historical News Report" summarizing the event chronologically.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies chronological events accurately.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 5 target vocabulary words appropriately (e.g., <i>independence, significance, transformation</i>).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies past simple and passive voice correctly.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is intelligible (especially -ed endings and contrastive sounds).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively listens and contributes to group discussion, asking for clarification when needed.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket before leaving:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List 3 new historical facts or vocabulary words you learned today from listening."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about listening to historical events, and how did you overcome it today?"</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you find easiest and most difficult about identifying chronological events in the audio today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can listening to historical narratives help you understand Panama's identity and culture better?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? Student engagement during the sequencing tasks and group discussions was high. The pronunciation practice on -ed endings was particularly effective. What adjustments are needed for the next lesson? Perhaps a slightly longer audio input next time, or more focused activities on reported speech. Some students still struggled with distinguishing /s/ and /z/ in fast speech, so a quick review might be beneficial. This lesson successfully introduced core vocabulary and listening for chronological events, preparing students to encounter these words in a reading context next.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The vocabulary and historical events explored in this listening lesson will be reinforced and expanded in Lesson 2, which will focus on reading comprehension of a text related to a similar historical theme, using the same set of target vocabulary.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>